  "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,e),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0,e) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061e\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{0}{e}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+\u0394x,e+\u0394y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\u0394f\u22481\\,\u0394x+0\\,\u0394y.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0,e)$ to $(1,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394x & =1-0=1, \\\\ \u0394y & =2-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394f & \u22481\\,\u0394x+0\\,\u0394y \\\\  & \u22481\\cdot (1)+0\\cdot (2-e) \\\\  & \u22481.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $1.$",
